<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android TV dimens, pinned.

  Same rule as colors_tv.xml: TV stops sharing measurements with the mobile tree,
  so a later edit to dimens.xml cannot move a TV control. Nothing here is used by
  a mobile layout, and nothing mobile may start using them.
-->
<resources>
    <!--
      Player back button inset, measured from the top and start edges of the
      1920x1080 frame to the *outer edge of the focus target*.

      The target itself is 48x48 and its focused state scales it 10%, so the
      inset has to clear the scaled box, not the resting one: 52.8dp grows 4.8dp
      in total, 2.4dp on each side, so 32 - 2.4 = 29.6dp (59px) is the closest
      any edge of the focused target comes to the frame. Measured on the TV AVD:
      focused bounds [59,59][165,165], resting box [64,64][160,160].

      It used to be flush with the corner (the button was placed by a 14dp/15dp
      margin that only had to position the 19dp glyph). A TV crops a few percent
      off each edge - the design guidance is 5%, 96px here - so a control pinned
      to the corner can be clipped or half off-screen entirely.
    -->
    <dimen name="tv_player_back_inset">32dp</dimen>

    <!--
      Player vertical metrics, and the reason they are dimens at all.

      The player's column is a chain whose every part but the artwork is a fixed
      height: artwork + 32 gap + 24 track line + 16 + 42 play/pause + 16 + 50 pill
      row + 60 bottom inset = 520dp. The bottom inset is never given up and the
      artwork is the only element the chain can move, so on any viewport shorter
      than 520dp the deficit came out of the artwork's top edge: measured clipped
      by 31dp at 1280x720@240 (480dp) and by 128dp at 1920x1080@480 (360dp), with
      the whole group riding up out of centre by the same amount.

      These values reproduce today's geometry exactly on every viewport that is
      520dp or taller, and give the artwork less room below that. The buckets are
      the Android way round for `-hNNNdp`: a device matches every bucket at or
      below its height and the largest one wins, so values-h520dp has to exist and
      has to repeat these numbers - without it, values-h480dp would win at 540dp
      and quietly shrink the artwork on a 1080p television.

      Below 360dp the qualifiers stop matching and this file's values apply, which
      is the one height range where the chain is still longer than the viewport.
      No television reports a viewport that short (the shortest measured is 360dp,
      an xxhdpi panel).
    -->
    <dimen name="tv_player_artwork_size">280dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="tv_player_artwork_gap">32dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="tv_player_bottom_inset">60dp</dimen>
</resources>
